# Chapter 6, fourth part: what is printed for each element of a document.
# Included by the chapter file.

.h2 What is printed for each element [print-elements]
.ix Elements, printed
.ix Typeset page
.ix Page layout, printed
.ix Long form of a marker
Each element of a document puts something on the page.  The rules below hold
for the PDF of Draft and Final and for the pages of a Preview alike; where a
Draft differs, the text says so.  The document is printed as it is in
the Editor, so that nothing has to be saved first; the parts that an Include
marker names are read from their files, so a part has to be saved
({n:marker-include-print}).

.h3 The page
.ix Paper size, printed
.ix Margins, printed
.ix Page number, printed
.ix Title page, printed
.table 19 41
Part of the page | What is printed
Paper size | The paper size of the Global Page Layout.  Without one, US
 letter, 8.5 by 11 inches.
Margins | The Left, Right, Top and Bottom Margin of the Global Page Layout, 1
 inch each when the document has none.  Margins that leave less than 2 inches
 for the text width or height are replaced by 1 inch.
Page number | A number centered at the foot of every page, when no header or
 footer of the document has text; if one has, the headers and the footers say
 what is printed ({n:format-page-headers}).  Its style is the
 Page Number Tag of the Global Page Layout: \1\ gives 1, 2, 3; \i\ gives i,
 ii, iii; \I\ gives I, II, III; \a\ gives a, b, c; \A\ gives A, B, C.  Any
 other tag gives 1, 2, 3.
Title page | The blocks of a structured document that come before its first
 level 1 section stand on a page of their own, with no page number.  They are
 set lower on the page by the Vertical Centering of the Title page layout (33
 percent when the document has none).  The numbering of the pages starts at 1
 after it.
Orientation | The Orientation marker ({n:markers}) turns the pages that follow
 to landscape from the block that holds it, and another one turns back.  At
 the end of the document the page is portrait.
Line numbers | A number in the margin of each printed line, in a small sans
 serif type, when the document asks for them (Global Format, line numbering,
 {n:format-fields-linenum}).  They are in a Print, a Preview and an exported
 LaTeX file, and never on the screen.
.end
The title page is made when the whole document is printed with Print Title
Page? at Yes (or a Preview of the Document with it).  A part of a document (a
section, a block, a range of sections) has no title page; its sections keep
their numbers.

.h3 Borders and shading [print-borders]
.ix Borders, printed
.ix Shading, printed
.ix Gutter, printed
A block that has borders is printed in a box: a thin line at each side that
has Single, two lines for Double and one thick line for Wide, at the sides of
each column, above and below the block and, in a table, between the columns.
The Top Gutter and the Bottom Gutter are room inside the box above and below
the text.  Shading fills the box with grey: 40 percent is a light grey, 100
percent is black, and in a print in black and white it stays grey.  Connect
Borders determines the space after the box: with Yes it is at most one line, so
that the boxes of blocks one after the other close up; with No it is one and a
half lines.  The Line Weight of Global Options is not used: the lines have the
same weight whatever its value is.  A headline has no border in the print.
Borders
are set with {k:Alt-F8} ({n:edit-c-border}).

The lines of a table meet as they do on the screen: the corners, the places
where a line between two rows meets a line at a side or between two columns,
and the crossings are closed for every kind of line, Single, Double and Wide,
and for a mixed table (Double lines above and below with Single lines at the
sides, or the reverse).  A vertical line runs through the space between the
two lines of a Double line, and a line between two columns is drawn once.
Where two neighbors disagree about a line that they share, the stronger kind
is drawn.

.h3 Headlines and section tags [print-tags]
.ix Section tags, printed
.ix Auto Numbering?
.ix Auto Numbering to Level
.ix Headlines, printed
A headline is set as a heading line: level 1 in a large type, level 2 in a
smaller large type and deeper levels in the size of the text, with the
attributes, justification and indents that the Format panels give to the
headline.  The space before it is the spacing of its level in the Global
Format settings (Headline, Subsection or Peer Section), and a headline is
kept together with the lines after it, so that it does not stand alone at
the foot of a page.  The tag that stands before the headline is the same tag
that the screen shows.  Three settings decide it.
.table 22 38
Setting | Effect on the printed tag
Auto Numbering? (Global Format) | With No, no headline has a tag.
Auto Numbering to Level (Global Format, a number, 99 by default) | A section
 deeper than that level has no tag.  With a value of 2, the headlines of
 levels 1 and 2 are numbered (1, 2.1) and those of level 3 and deeper are not.
 A level that has a Section Tag of its own keeps it whatever this setting
 says.
Section Tag (a field of every Level panel) | A text in which \1\, \i\, \I\,
 \a\ and \A\ are replaced by the number of the section among the sections of
 its own level, as arabic, lower case roman, upper case roman, lower case
 letters or upper case letters.  A roman numeral shows the numbers 1 to
 3999; above that the number is arabic.  Everything else is printed as
 typed, the blanks at the end included.
.end
Without a Section Tag the tag is the numbers of the section and of all the
sections above it, joined by points and followed by a blank: 1, 1.1, 1.1.6.
The tag belongs to the headlines of a structured document; the headline of
the root section, the title, has none.

A section that is printed on its own (Print Current Section Only? or a Preview
of a Section) keeps the tag that it has in the whole document, at its own
level: the tags of a part are the tags that the screen shows, and Auto
Numbering to Level counts the depth of the section in the document.  The same
holds for a headline that has columns (a side head): its tag stands at the
start of the text of its first column.  The tag is made for sections down to
the 99th level, the deepest that a document has.

.h3 Text, justification and spacing
.ix Text, printed
.ix Justification, printed
.ix Line spacing, printed
.ix Attributes, printed
.table 19 41
Element | What is printed
Justification | Left, right, center and even, as the Format panels give for
 the block: even gives the justified text of TeX, with hyphenation.
Indents | The first line and the following lines, and the left and right
 margins of the block, in the units of the Ruler of the Global Format settings
 (a unit is one eightieth of the text width, or a tenth, a twelfth or a
 fifteenth of an inch: {n:format-fields-spacing}).
Text indent character | A hanging indent of the paragraph.
Attributes | Bold, italic and underscore are printed (underscore of words and
 of all text as an underline).
Line spacing | The Line spacing of the Global Format settings in a Final, or
 that of the block when its named format has one.  A Draft uses one line.
 Print Double Spaced? doubles it in both.
Space between blocks | The Block spacing of the Global Format settings, or that
 of the block when its named format has one, in a Final, and the space before
 and after headlines.  Only a spacing of more than one line adds space.  A
 Draft has none.
Pages | A page starts at each headline of the Page Break Level of the Global
 Format settings and of the levels above it, in a Final and in a Draft.
Non-breaking space | A space that is not a place to break the line.
Tab characters | The tab stops of the format, with the fill character (dots)
 of the tab.  The tab stops are the least exact part: they are approximated.
Characters | The characters of the document are printed as the letters and
 signs that they stand for (accented letters, Greek letters, signs, box
 drawing, arrows, card suits).  A character that no font has is printed as
 [U+XXXX] in a small typewriter type and reported as a print error
 ({n:print-errkinds}).
.end

.h3 Tables
.ix Tables, printed
.ix Table rows
.ix Borders, printed
A run of blocks with the same columns is a table.  It is printed as one table
with a column for each column of the blocks, and the rows break across pages
as TeX determines.  The width of a column is its width in the units of the Ruler
of the Global Format settings; the columns are laid out from the left, and a
column that would pass the right edge of the text gets the width that is left.  The borders of a column (left, top,
right, bottom) are drawn as rules.  The alignment of a cell is that of its
column.  A block with several columns whose rows have other widths starts a
new table.  Outside tables the text of a block runs in one column.

.h3 Notes
.ix Notes, printed
.ix Footnotes, printed
.ix Levelnotes, printed
.ix Endnotes, printed
The Note marker ({n:markers}) is printed by the settings of the Footnote,
Levelnote and Endnote panels ({n:format}).
.table 13 47
Kind | Where it is printed
Footnote | At the foot of the page that holds the mark.  The mark in the text
 and the number of the note are set by the Note Format panel (tags,
 attributes), and the text of the note is set as one paragraph in a smaller
 type, numbered with its tag.  A note in a table cell or in a box (a block
 with borders or shading) is set at the foot of the page after the table or
 the box.
Levelnote | After the last block of the section of the level that the Note
 Level setting names (the end of the level 1 section when the level is 1), or
 of the section that holds the note when its level number is smaller.

Endnote | In a list at the end of the document, on a page of its own, under
 the title of the Note panel.
.end
The text of a note is printed as one paragraph; the line ends of the note are
blanks.  A note in a Draft or a Final is printed alike.

.h3 Markers
.ix Markers, printed
.ix Long form, markers
.ix Comment, printed in a draft
Each marker is printed in one of three ways: it is typeset (its effect is
done), it is replaced by its value, or it is not typeset and is printed in
its {b:long form}: the label of the marker in the Long display mode, in a
small typewriter type, in a box or in brackets, so that the page shows that it
was there.  This table lists every marker of {n:markers}.
.table 15 45
Marker | Printed
Note | A footnote, a levelnote or an endnote (above).
Cross-reference | The tag of the section, or its headline, as the marker says.
 A reference to a page prints the page number.  A reference that cannot be
 resolved is printed in its long form.
Index | Nothing when the index is made ({n:print-index}); the long form when the
 index is off.
Keep | Nothing: a Keep marker is never printed.  It keeps the blocks between a
 Start and an End on one page ({n:print-keep}).
Author, Description | The Author and the Description of the document (the Save
 panel of {n:file}).
Revision | The revision that the marker shows.
Date, Time | The date and the time of the print, in the format of the marker.
Filedate, Filetime | The date and the time of the file of the document.
Filename, Pathname | The name of the document and its full path.
Printer | The name of the printer.
Page Number | The number of the page, in the style of the Page Number Tag.  It
 belongs to the headers and the footers ({n:format-page-headers}).
Section | The number and the headline of the section that the page is in, in one
 of the three forms of the marker.  It belongs to the headers and the footers
 ({n:format-page-headers}).
Comment | Nothing in a Final.  In a Draft the label of the comment in
 brackets, with its author, its time and its text.
Equation | The equation typeset as TeX math (below).
Figure, Table | Nothing when the list of figures or of tables is made
 ({n:print-index}); the long form when the list is off.
Font | Changes the family of the type (serif, sans serif, typewriter) up to
 the end of the block, by the name of the font ({n:print-fonts}).  A
 Font marker with no number goes back to the font of the block.
Include | The text of the document that the marker names, at the place of the
 marker ({n:marker-include-print}).  An Include that cannot be made prints a
 notice in square brackets ({n:marker-include-errors}).
Merge | The long form.
If, Else, Endif | Nothing: the markers are not printed.  The text of the
 branch that the condition chooses is printed and the text of the other is not
 ({n:marker-if-print}).
Counter | The value of the counter, as the Counter Format panel and the marker
 say.
Line | A rule or a string repeated to the length, as the marker says: the
 string, the length in inches and the weight (a weight is cut to between 3 and
 20 hundredths of an inch).
Page Break | A page break.  The field of the marker may ask for the next odd
 page or the next even page.  Inside a table cell or a box (a block with
 borders or shading) it is printed in the long form.
Column Break | A page break.  In a table cell or a box, the long form.
Whitespace | A vertical space of the size and the unit of the marker.  Size 0
 prints nothing.  In a table cell or a box the space is a rule of that height.
Orientation | See the page above.
Picture | The picture (below).  A picture that cannot be used is printed in
 its long form.
Keycap | The legend in a box like a key, with the colors, the scale and the
 font of the marker.  A keycap is always a word in a line of text.
LaTeX | The text of the marker, as it was typed, at the place of the marker
 ({n:marker-latex}).
.end
.note A marker that is printed in its long form is reported as a print error,
  so that the Error Log and the Print Error markers tell of each piece that
  could not be printed as it was ({n:print-errors}).

.h3 Pictures
.ix Pictures, printed
.ix PNG, JPEG, PDF, EPS, printed
.ix Picture lookup
The Picture marker names a file.  The file is looked for with these rules.
.table 20 40
Name | Where it is looked for
A full path | As written.
A name with a directory | Below the directory of the document, as written.  A
 backslash is the same as a slash.
A name with no directory | In the directory of the document and then in the
 working directory, comparing the name without regard to the case of the
 letters.
.end
PNG, JPEG, PDF and EPS (PostScript) files are drawn, whatever the ending of
their name.  A PNG or a JPEG file that is cut short is not used.  The
size of the picture is the Width and Height of the marker (the aspect is kept
when both are given), or the scale of the marker, or the natural size.  A
picture wider than the line or higher than 85 percent of the text area is
scaled down to fit.
A picture that has no file, a file of another kind, or a file that is not
whole is printed in its long form, and the run goes on.  A Final and a Draft
print keep the colors of a picture and print them in color on a color
printer, whatever the kind of file; with {b:Print in Black and White?} the
picture is printed in greys.

.h3 Equations
.ix Equations, printed
.ix TeX math, printed
.ix Display equation
An Equation marker holds TeX math ({n:equations}).  It is set as TeX math: as a
display equation, centered on a line of its own, when it is alone on its line
(the other things on that line being blanks, tabs and attribute changes), and
inline otherwise.  In a table cell or in a box (a block with borders or shading) an equation is
always inline.  A text
that LaTeX would reject, or that uses a command that is not allowed (reading
or writing files, and the like), is not typeset: the equation is printed in its
long form, with its text, and the run goes on and gives a PDF. 

.h3 Keep markers: blocks on one page [print-keep]
.ix Keep, printed
.ix Keep Start (Fixed)
.ix Keep Start (Floating)
.ix Blocks on one page
A Keep marker is never printed.  What it does is to keep blocks on one page.
The blocks from a Start to the End are a region, set so that the whole
region is on one page.  The rules for the markers are these.
.table 22 38
Case | Result
Start and End | The blocks between them are kept on one page.  The first End
 closes the region.
A Start inside a region | Ignored.
An End with no Start | Ignored.
A Start with no End | Keeps to the end of the document.
A region across sections | It is broken into one part for each section; each
 part is kept on a page, and a headline goes with the kept block that follows
 it.
A marker in the text of a block | The unit is the whole block.
A marker in a row of a table | The unit is the whole table.
A block that holds Keep markers only | Not printed at all and takes no height.
A part that holds a Page Break, a Column Break or an Orientation marker | Not
 kept: these change the page inside the part, so the Keep markers are dropped
 and the text is set as if there were none.
A part that begins in the text of the title page | Not kept.
.end
.table 17 43
Marker | What it does
Start (Fixed) | If the region does not fit in the rest of the page, a new page
 is started and the region is placed there.  The page that was left short ends
 early; the text after the End stays after the region.
Start (Floating) | If the region does not fit in the rest of the page, it goes
 to the top of the next page and the text after the End goes on in this page,
 in front of it.  A region that fits is put where it stands.
.end
A region that is taller than the text area of a page (or, for a floating
one, taller than nine tenths of it) cannot be kept: it starts on a new page
and then breaks between lines and rows as usual.  Nothing is lost.  The
footnotes of the blocks in a region are printed on the page where the region is
placed.  A table in a region is a row at a time, with the same widths and
borders, so that it can be broken if it does not fit.  The Editor still shows
the label of the marker on the screen.  The format field Keep Block on One
Page? is another feature ({n:format}).
